["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Each grouse lives in a band of the mountain with its own trees and foods. Find the band, find the food, find the bird.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cross section of one slope from valley to peak. Grass and sage at the bottom: sharp tailed grouse, open only in MUs 3-30 and 3-31. Aspen and creeks: ruffed grouse, most common at 750 to 1,250 metres. Fir and pine: dusky grouse in summer, and spruce grouse. Spruce and subalpine fir: spruce grouse, and dusky grouse in winter. Alpine at the top: white tailed ptarmigan.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"320\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">One slope, five grouse homes</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"40\" width=\"400\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#f4f4f0\"/>\n<polygon points=\"20,88 40,50 60,88\" fill=\"#b9b3a6\"/><polygon points=\"33,64 40,50 47,64\" fill=\"#ffffff\"/>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Alpine, above trees</text>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">White tailed ptarmigan</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"94\" width=\"400\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#c9d4bd\"/>\n<polygon points=\"30,106 14.7,140 45.3,140\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"27\" y=\"140\" width=\"6\" height=\"5\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<polygon points=\"52,112 39.4,140 64.6,140\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"49\" y=\"140\" width=\"6\" height=\"4\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Spruce, subalpine fir</text>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Spruce; dusky in winter</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"148\" width=\"400\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#d6dec8\"/>\n<polygon points=\"30,160 14.7,194 45.3,194\" fill=\"#46613d\"/><rect x=\"27\" y=\"194\" width=\"6\" height=\"5\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<polygon points=\"52,166 39.4,194 64.6,194\" fill=\"#46613d\"/><rect x=\"49\" y=\"194\" width=\"6\" height=\"4\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Douglas fir and pine</text>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dusky in summer; spruce</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"202\" width=\"400\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#e8ead6\"/>\n<rect x=\"27\" y=\"234\" width=\"6\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#d8d2c4\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><ellipse cx=\"30\" cy=\"226\" rx=\"12\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#e9c46a\"/>\n<rect x=\"49\" y=\"237\" width=\"6\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#d8d2c4\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><ellipse cx=\"52\" cy=\"230\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"12\" fill=\"#e9c46a\"/>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"224\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Aspen, creek draws</text>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"244\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Ruffed: 750 to 1,250 m</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"256\" width=\"400\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#efe6cf\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"32\" cy=\"296\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#b8b08a\"/><ellipse cx=\"54\" cy=\"298\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"5\" fill=\"#b8b08a\"/>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Grass and sage</text>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"298\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sharp tailed: MUs 3-30, 3-31</text>\n<path d=\"M300 310 L330 260 L350 200 L370 140 L385 80 L395 40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n<text x=\"398\" y=\"312\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\">Uphill</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Grass, aspen, fir, spruce, alpine: one grouse for each (80 to 85%).</li></ul>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 328\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Elevation chart in metres, 85 percent, BC Breeding Bird Atlas. Ruffed grouse are most abundant from 750 to 1,250 metres. Dusky grouse breed from about 500 to 2,250 metres. Spruce grouse live up to 2,500 metres, drawn as a dashed bar because no lower limit is given. White tailed ptarmigan breed from 1,830 to 2,650 metres in the Interior. Ruffed grouse like aspen near water; spruce grouse like pine and spruce.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"328\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">How high they live in BC (85%)</text>\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"392\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 4\"/>\n  <text x=\"54\" y=\"275.0\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text>\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"231\" x2=\"392\" y2=\"231\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 4\"/>\n  <text x=\"54\" y=\"235.71428571428572\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">500</text>\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"191\" x2=\"392\" y2=\"191\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 4\"/>\n  <text x=\"54\" y=\"196.42857142857144\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">1,000</text>\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"152\" x2=\"392\" y2=\"152\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 4\"/>\n  <text x=\"54\" y=\"157.14285714285714\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">1,500</text>\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"113\" x2=\"392\" y2=\"113\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 4\"/>\n  <text x=\"54\" y=\"117.85714285714286\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">2,000</text>\n  <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"74\" x2=\"392\" y2=\"74\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 4\"/>\n  <text x=\"54\" y=\"78.57142857142858\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">2,500</text>\n  <text x=\"18\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 18 160)\">metres</text>\n  <rect x=\"80\" y=\"172\" width=\"54\" height=\"39\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n  <text x=\"107\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ruffed</text>\n  <text x=\"107\" y=\"165.78571428571428\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">750 to 1,250</text>\n  <rect x=\"160\" y=\"93\" width=\"54\" height=\"138\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#4b4f55\"/>\n  <text x=\"187\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Dusky</text>\n  <text x=\"187\" y=\"87.21428571428572\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">500 to 2,250</text>\n  <rect x=\"240\" y=\"74\" width=\"54\" height=\"196\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 5\"/>\n  <rect x=\"240\" y=\"74\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n  <text x=\"267\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Spruce</text>\n  <text x=\"267\" y=\"67.57142857142858\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">to 2,500 m</text>\n  <rect x=\"320\" y=\"62\" width=\"54\" height=\"64\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/>\n  <text x=\"347\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ptarmigan</text>\n  <text x=\"347\" y=\"55.78571428571428\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1,830 to 2,650</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"318\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ruffed: aspen near water. Spruce: pine, spruce</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the bands with numbers</summary><ul><li>Sharp tailed grouse: grassland and open forest, breeding at 275 to 1,190 m in BC (85%, BC species account).</li><li>Ruffed grouse: most abundant at 750 to 1,250 m, aspen and poplar near water (85%, BC Breeding Bird Atlas).</li><li>Dusky grouse: breed mainly in Douglas fir and spruce fir zones, many seen at 1,500 to 2,250 m, but breeding at 500 to 1,250 m is not uncommon <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Spruce grouse: conifer forest with spruce or lodgepole pine, recorded up to 2,500 m <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>White tailed ptarmigan: alpine, breeding at 1,830 to 2,650 m in the Interior <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-aspen-grove-snow.jpg\" alt=\"Aspen grove: buds all winter.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Aspen grove: buds all winter. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jason Hollinger, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aspen_Grove_in_Snow_(2276157824).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-dogwood-flowers.jpg\" alt=\"Red osier dogwood: white berries in fall.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Red osier dogwood: white berries in fall. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Robert Flogaus-Faust, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cornus_sericea_RF.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-chokecherry.jpg\" alt=\"Chokecherry in fruit.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Chokecherry in fruit. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thayne Tuason, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prunus_virginiana_fruittwo-9-08-04.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-wells-gray-cutblock.jpg\" alt=\"Young regrowth in a cut block.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Young regrowth in a cut block. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jason Hollinger, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Big_Bertha_(3818285480).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Young forest under about 20 years, aspen, creek bottoms (75 to 85%).</li><li>Edges where two kinds of cover meet (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: food and cover through the year</summary><ul><li>Young forest less than 20 years old gives food, hiding cover and shelter (75%, Pennsylvania Game Commission).</li><li>Aspen, poplar, birch and alder near water <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Aspen flower buds are the top winter food <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Fall foods: berries, clover, leaves and seeds <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Fruit is over a quarter of the adult diet <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Winter roosts under conifers when snow is poor (75 to 85%).</li><li>Home range a few hectares; adults rarely move more than a few hundred metres a day (75 to 85%).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-subalpine-forest.jpg\" alt=\"Spruce and lodgepole pine, Manning Park.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Spruce and lodgepole pine, Manning Park. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Tim Gage at Flickr, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Picea_engelmannii_Pinus_contorta.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-kinnikinnick.jpg\" alt=\"Kinnikinnick berries under pine.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Kinnikinnick berries under pine. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin from Bozeman, Montana, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arctostaphylos_uva-ursi_(5192873642).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-huckleberry-bush.jpg\" alt=\"Huckleberry on a rocky ridge.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Huckleberry on a rocky ridge. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Katja Schulz from Washington, D. C., USA, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Huckleberry_(14891686107).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Dense spruce, fir and lodgepole pine (80 to 85%).</li><li>On the ground in summer, up in the trees from late fall <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: food and cover</summary><ul><li>Conifer specialist: needles of pine or spruce, especially in winter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Summer and fall: green shoots, leaves, berries, flowers and insects (75%, Idaho Fish and Game).</li><li>Mostly on the ground from late spring to early autumn, then up in the trees eating needles (80%, Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife).</li><li>Some move up to about 9.5 km between summer and winter range <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>East of the Coast Mountains in BC <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-douglas-fir-stand.jpg\" alt=\"Open Douglas fir: dusky winter needles.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Open Douglas fir: dusky winter needles. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin from Bozeman, Montana, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pseudotsuga_menziesii_subsp._glauca_(Interior_Douglas-fir),_Wild_Horse_Island,_Montana_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-huckleberry-berry.jpg\" alt=\"Huckleberry: late summer food.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Huckleberry: late summer food. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: julie from Idaho, united states, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaccinium_membranaceum_Fruit_and_leaves.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-grass-fir-edge.jpg\" alt=\"Grass and fir edge: summer and early fall.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Grass and fir edge: summer and early fall. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Andres Alvarado, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_colorful_of_Lac_Du_Bois_Grasslands.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Summer: open edges, even sagebrush. Fall: uphill into dense conifers (70 to 75%).</li><li>Sooty grouse on the coast winter high in conifers <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: food and cover</summary><ul><li>Breeding season: forest edges and openings; insects, green plants and berries (75%, Idaho Fish and Game).</li><li>Summer berries: blueberry, huckleberry, chokecherry, gooseberry; grasshoppers <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Autumn: most birds move from open breeding areas to dense conifer forest, usually uphill (70%, Audubon). Maximum known travel about 48 km (30 miles) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Winter: fir and Douglas fir needles, perched high in the trees (65 to 75%).</li><li>Sooty grouse: winter in dense conifers, often higher than their breeding ground; sources disagree in places, so treat direction as likely, not certain (55 to 65%, <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> near Mission).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-lac-du-bois-grassland.jpg\" alt=\"Lac du Bois grassland near Kamloops.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Lac du Bois grassland near Kamloops. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: tuchodi, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lac_du_Bois_Grasslands_Protected_Area_(4043429977).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-kamloops-sagebrush.jpg\" alt=\"Sagebrush and bunchgrass.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Sagebrush and bunchgrass. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Ghune, CC0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grassland.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-woods-rose-hips.jpg\" alt=\"Rose hips: a key fall and winter food.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Rose hips: a key fall and winter food. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jim Morefield from Nevada, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_wildrose,_Rosa_woodsii_(16469499462).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Grassland with shrubs, aspen and water birch draws <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Region 3: only MUs (Management Units) 3-30 and 3-31 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: food, cover and the rule</summary><ul><li>Key grassland foods in BC: snowberry, rose and dandelion; browse on water birch, trembling aspen, saskatoon and chokecherry (85%, BC species account).</li><li>Birds in clearcuts use kinnikinnick, juniper, prickly rose and scrub birch <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Fall: roadsides and landings with clover, dandelion and yarrow are used heavily <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Winter: riparian draws with buds, catkins and berries <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Late fall and winter birds may feed high in aspens and birches (75%, Alaska Department of Fish and Game).</li><li>Rule: \"SHARP-TAILED GROUSE | 3-30, 3-31 | Sept 10 - Nov 30 | 5 (10)\", and no open season in the part of 3-30 south of the Scottie Creek Forest Service Road, Map C18 (99%, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-3/12\">Rule Book</a>).</li><li>Lac du Bois Grasslands Protected Area: hunting allowed in open season, but parts sit inside City of Kamloops limits and have their own closures (85 to 95%, earlier research). Check the map layers before you go.</li></ul></details><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-ptarmigan-summer.jpg\" alt=\"White tailed ptarmigan on alpine rock.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>White tailed ptarmigan on alpine rock. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS - Pacific Region, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Rainier_White-Tailed_Ptarmigan_(53058740798).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-willow-shrub.jpg\" alt=\"Willow: the core winter food.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Willow: the core winter food. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin from Bozeman, Montana, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salix_bebbiana_(5002370825).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Above the trees: rock, heather, willow basins <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Winter: willow buds, sheltered basins at treeline (75 to 85%).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: food and cover</summary><ul><li>White tailed ptarmigan are strictly alpine; they raise young in moist pockets among cliffs and snowfields (85%, Hinterland Who's Who).</li><li>Winter: buds and twigs of low willow, alder and dwarf birch; white tailed also eat conifer needles and buds <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>In winter they move a few hundred metres to a few kilometres to reliable food <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Region 3 ptarmigan row: MUs 3-12 to 3-20 and 3-26 to 3-44, 1 September to 30 November, 5 (15) <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>. Region 2: listed MUs only. Region 8: no row <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>Sun Peaks, MU 3-27: no hunting or shooting within 400 m of any lift, run, facility or development (99%, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-3/31\">Rule Book</a>).</li><li>Region 3 above 1,700 m: vehicles only on existing roads and trails (99%, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-3/22\">Rule Book</a>).</li></ul></details><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Bird</th><th>Fall</th><th>Winter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ruffed</td><td>Clover, berries, leaves</td><td>Aspen buds, catkins</td></tr><tr><td>Spruce</td><td>Berries, shoots</td><td>Pine, spruce needles</td></tr><tr><td>Dusky, sooty</td><td>Berries, then needles</td><td>Fir needles</td></tr><tr><td>Sharp tailed</td><td>Greens, berries</td><td>Buds, catkins</td></tr><tr><td>Ptarmigan</td><td>Leaves, berries</td><td>Willow buds</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Open crops to see what they eat this week (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: sources for the table</summary><ul><li>Ruffed: Hinterland Who's Who; Pennsylvania Game Commission (75 to 85%).</li><li>Spruce: Idaho Fish and Game; Pendergast and Boag <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Dusky and sooty: Idaho Fish and Game; Audubon; Birdweb (65 to 75%).</li><li>Sharp tailed: BC species account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Ptarmigan: Hinterland Who's Who <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Line chart of elevation by month, a general pattern drawn from BC atlas ranges, not tracking data. Dusky grouse: low on breeding ground in April to July around 1,100 metres, then uphill through fall to dense conifers near 1,900 metres all winter. Ruffed grouse: stay at about the same height all year, around 1,000 metres. White tailed ptarmigan: high all year, dropping a little toward treeline in winter.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Height through the year (general pattern)</text>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"50\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"46\" y=\"275.0\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\">500 m</text>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"270.0\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"270.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"46\" y=\"225.0\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\">1 km</text>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"220.0\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"220.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"46\" y=\"175.0\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\">1.5 km</text>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"170.0\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"170.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"46\" y=\"125.0\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\">2 km</text>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"120.0\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"120.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"46\" y=\"75.0\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\">2.5 km</text>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"70.0\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#e5e5e5\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64.16666666666667\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text>\n<text x=\"92.5\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">F</text>\n<text x=\"120.83333333333333\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text>\n<text x=\"149.16666666666669\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text>\n<text x=\"177.5\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text>\n<text x=\"205.83333333333334\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text>\n<text x=\"234.16666666666666\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text>\n<text x=\"262.5\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text>\n<text x=\"290.83333333333337\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">S</text>\n<text x=\"319.1666666666667\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text>\n<text x=\"347.5\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">N</text>\n<text x=\"375.8333333333333\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">D</text>\n<polyline points=\"64.2,130.0 92.5,130.0 120.8,140.0 149.2,200.0 177.5,210.0 205.8,210.0 234.2,200.0 262.5,170.0 290.8,150.0 319.2,135.0 347.5,130.0 375.8,130.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n<polyline points=\"64.2,220.0 92.5,220.0 120.8,220.0 149.2,220.0 177.5,220.0 205.8,220.0 234.2,220.0 262.5,220.0 290.8,220.0 319.2,220.0 347.5,220.0 375.8,220.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8b5a2b\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n<polyline points=\"64.2,110.0 92.5,110.0 120.8,100.0 149.2,90.0 177.5,85.0 205.8,80.0 234.2,80.0 262.5,80.0 290.8,85.0 319.2,90.0 347.5,100.0 375.8,110.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 4\"/>\n<text x=\"177.5\" y=\"232.0\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dusky</text>\n<text x=\"319.1666666666667\" y=\"242.0\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Ruffed</text>\n<text x=\"234.16666666666666\" y=\"70.0\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">White tailed ptarmigan</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Dusky climbs in fall and winters high (70 to 75%).</li><li>Ruffed stays put; ptarmigan stay high (80 to 85%).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: how to use it</summary><ul><li>The chart is a general pattern from BC atlas ranges and agency accounts, not tracking data from BC birds (Tip).</li><li>October: start low on dusky breeding slopes, then work up to the first dense fir ridges (Tip).</li><li>Ruffed grouse do not migrate; young birds disperse a few kilometres in fall <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Use the <a href=\"#/map\">Hunt Map</a> elevation profile to plan the height you walk (next screens).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Grouse</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Heffley Creek, MU 3-27</td><td>Ruffed, spruce, dusky, ptarmigan up high</td><td>Region 3 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Mission, Region 2</td><td>Sooty, ruffed, spruce in the mountains</td><td>Many shooting closures <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></td></tr><tr><td>South Okanagan, Region 8</td><td>Ruffed, spruce, dusky</td><td>No sharp tail, no ptarmigan <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: detail by area</summary><ul><li>Heffley Creek, Heffley Lake and Sun Peaks sit in MU (Management Unit) 3-27 on the 2026 Region 3 map <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Near Heffley Creek: the Douglas fir and aspen benches above the valley hold ruffed grouse; higher pine and spruce toward Heffley Lake and Sun Peaks hold spruce and dusky grouse (Tip, 65%).</li><li>Sharp tailed grouse are not open in 3-27; nearest open MUs are 3-30 and 3-31 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>Mission, MU 2-8: many no shooting areas along roads and near town; check <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-2/41\">Rule Book Region 2</a> before any trip <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>South Okanagan, Region 8: all three forest grouse, 5 a day in total (99%, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-8/10\">Rule Book</a>).</li><li>See also <a href=\"#/s/local-heffley\">Heffley Creek</a>, <a href=\"#/s/local-mission\">Mission</a> and <a href=\"#/s/local-okanagan\">Okanagan</a>.</li></ul></details><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside>","<div class=\"steps\" data-n=\"4\"><div class=\"steps-head\"><b>Grouse ground on the Hunt Map in 4 steps</b><span class=\"steps-count\">1 of 4</span></div><div class=\"steps-track\"><figure class=\"step-frame\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Map step 1. Turn on the Management Units layer, a dashed MU line, and the Forest Service roads layer, orange road lines with a spur.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Step 1: MU and Forest Service roads</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"40\" width=\"380\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#eef1e6\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<path d=\"M20 60 Q150 90 200 70 T380 90\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"10 6\"/>\n<text x=\"30\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">MU line</text>\n<path d=\"M30 240 Q120 200 180 170 Q240 140 300 160 Q350 175 380 130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"5\"/>\n<path d=\"M180 170 Q200 210 260 235\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<text x=\"240\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">FSR</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Layers: Management Units, Forest Service roads</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>1.</b> Turn on Management Units and Forest Service roads.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"step-frame\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Map step 2. Turn on Cutblocks by age and Burns by year. Young cutblocks around 10 to 20 years old, older blocks over 20 years, and an old burn show where young forest grows.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Step 2: young forest and burns</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"40\" width=\"380\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#eef1e6\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<polygon points=\"60,90 150,80 160,150 70,160\" fill=\"#e9c46a\" opacity=\".8\"/><polygon points=\"220,170 320,160 330,230 230,236\" fill=\"#8fbf6a\" opacity=\".8\"/><polygon points=\"250,60 360,70 350,130 260,120\" fill=\"#9aa0a6\" opacity=\".6\"/>\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">10 to 20 yr</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">20 yr +</text>\n<text x=\"305\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Old burn</text>\n<path d=\"M30 240 Q120 200 180 170 Q240 140 300 160 Q350 175 380 130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Layers: Cutblocks by age, Burns by year</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>2.</b> Add Cutblocks by age and Burns by year.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"step-frame\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Map step 3. Turn on the Slope direction terrain tool. North faces are cool and hold conifers. South and east faces are warm, where aspen and berry shrubs often grow. Then check Sun and shade.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Step 3: slope direction (aspect)</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"40\" width=\"380\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#eef1e6\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"145\" rx=\"150\" ry=\"85\" fill=\"#cfd8c0\"/><ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"145\" rx=\"90\" ry=\"50\" fill=\"#b8c4a4\"/><ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"145\" rx=\"35\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"#a3b08c\"/>\n<path d=\"M200 145 L200 60 A150 85 0 0 0 52 135 Z\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" opacity=\".25\"/><path d=\"M200 145 L348 150 A150 85 0 0 1 200 230 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".3\"/>\n<text x=\"130\" y=\"95\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">North: cool, conifer</text>\n<text x=\"280\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">South, east: warm</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Terrain: Slope direction, then Sun and shade</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>3.</b> Use Slope direction, then Sun and shade.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"step-frame\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Map step 4. Use the Elevation profile tool along your route. The shaded band from 750 to 1,250 metres is where ruffed grouse are most common in BC.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Step 4: walk the right height</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"40\" width=\"380\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#eef1e6\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<path d=\"M20 230 L90 200 L160 150 L230 120 L300 90 L380 60\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"130\" width=\"370\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".15\"/>\n<text x=\"380\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">1,250 m</text>\n<text x=\"380\" y=\"186\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">750 m</text>\n<text x=\"30\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Ruffed band</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Tool: Elevation profile along your route</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>4.</b> Check the Elevation profile for the right band.</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"steps-nav\"><button class=\"steps-prev\" aria-label=\"Back\">‹ Back</button><span class=\"steps-dots\"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><button class=\"steps-next\" aria-label=\"Next\">Next ›</button></div></div><ul><li>Open the <a href=\"#/map\">Hunt Map</a>. Layers first, terrain tools second (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what each tool shows for grouse</summary><ul><li>Management Units: confirm the MU and that grouse are open there (99% rule; map layer 95%).</li><li>Forest Service roads: access, and where the road rules apply. Read <a href=\"#/s/mc-grouse-09/6\">How to hunt</a> first.</li><li>Cutblocks by age: young regrowth roughly 10 to 20 years old is ruffed grouse cover (Tip, based on Pennsylvania's under 20 years, 75%).</li><li>Burns by year: older burns regrow aspen and shrubs (Tip).</li><li>Private land, parks, reserves and Motor vehicle closures: check every one before you go (99% rules).</li><li>Slope direction (aspect): south and east faces warm first; aspen benches often sit there in the Interior (Tip).</li><li>Sun and shade: morning sun on a slope can bring birds out to feed (Tip).</li><li>Viewshed: less useful for grouse; use it to plan where a partner can see you (Tip).</li><li>Elevation profile: ruffed 750 to 1,250 m; dusky and spruce higher; ptarmigan above the trees <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cross section of one slope from valley to peak. Grass and sage at the bottom: sharp tailed grouse, open only in MUs 3-30 and 3-31. Aspen and creeks: ruffed grouse, most common at 750 to 1,250 metres. Fir and pine: dusky grouse in summer, and spruce grouse. Spruce and subalpine fir: spruce grouse, and dusky grouse in winter. Alpine at the top: white tailed ptarmigan.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"320\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">One slope, five grouse homes</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"40\" width=\"400\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#f4f4f0\"/>\n<polygon points=\"20,88 40,50 60,88\" fill=\"#b9b3a6\"/><polygon points=\"33,64 40,50 47,64\" fill=\"#ffffff\"/>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Alpine, above trees</text>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">White tailed ptarmigan</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"94\" width=\"400\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#c9d4bd\"/>\n<polygon points=\"30,106 14.7,140 45.3,140\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"27\" y=\"140\" width=\"6\" height=\"5\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<polygon points=\"52,112 39.4,140 64.6,140\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"49\" y=\"140\" width=\"6\" height=\"4\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Spruce, subalpine fir</text>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Spruce; dusky in winter</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"148\" width=\"400\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#d6dec8\"/>\n<polygon points=\"30,160 14.7,194 45.3,194\" fill=\"#46613d\"/><rect x=\"27\" y=\"194\" width=\"6\" height=\"5\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<polygon points=\"52,166 39.4,194 64.6,194\" fill=\"#46613d\"/><rect x=\"49\" y=\"194\" width=\"6\" height=\"4\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Douglas fir and pine</text>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dusky in summer; spruce</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"202\" width=\"400\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#e8ead6\"/>\n<rect x=\"27\" y=\"234\" width=\"6\" height=\"20\" fill=\"#d8d2c4\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><ellipse cx=\"30\" cy=\"226\" rx=\"12\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#e9c46a\"/>\n<rect x=\"49\" y=\"237\" width=\"6\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#d8d2c4\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><ellipse cx=\"52\" cy=\"230\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"12\" fill=\"#e9c46a\"/>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"224\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Aspen, creek draws</text>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"244\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Ruffed: 750 to 1,250 m</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"256\" width=\"400\" height=\"54\" fill=\"#efe6cf\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"32\" cy=\"296\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#b8b08a\"/><ellipse cx=\"54\" cy=\"298\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"5\" fill=\"#b8b08a\"/>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Grass and sage</text>\n<text x=\"78\" y=\"298\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sharp tailed: MUs 3-30, 3-31</text>\n<path d=\"M300 310 L330 260 L350 200 L370 140 L385 80 L395 40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n<text x=\"398\" y=\"312\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\">Uphill</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Rhyme: \"Grass for the sharp tail, aspen for the ruff, fir for the dusky, spruce for the tough, rock for the ptarmigan high enough.\"</li><li>Number trick: \"750 to 1,250\": the ruffed band.</li><li>Old hand's rule (tradition): \"Hunt the edges, not the middle.\"</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: more hooks</summary><ul><li>\"Under 20 years, grouse appears\": young forest.</li><li>\"Dusky goes up, ruffed stays home.\"</li><li>\"No sharp tail in 3-27.\"</li><li>The edge rule is a classic piece of grouse hunting advice; the rest are made for this course.</li></ul></details>","<ul><li>BC Breeding Bird Atlas, BC species account, Hinterland Who's Who, Pennsylvania Game Commission, Rule Book. Checked 2026-10-11.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: every source</summary><ul><li>Rule Book: <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-3/12\">Region 3 grouse</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-3/22\">above 1,700 m</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-3/31\">Sun Peaks</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-2/41\">Region 2</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-8/10\">Region 8</a>. Synopsis 2 October 2026 edition <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>BC Breeding Bird Atlas accounts for ruffed, spruce, dusky grouse and white tailed ptarmigan, read 2026-10-01 to 2026-10-03 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Site down on 2026-10-11.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/frpa/iwms/documents/Birds/b_columbiansharptailedgrouse.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC species account, Columbian Sharp tailed Grouse</a>, read directly <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.hww.ca/en/wildlife/birds/ruffed-grouse.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hinterland Who's Who, Ruffed grouse</a> and <a href=\"https://www.hww.ca/assets/pdfs/factsheets/ptarmigan-en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ptarmigan</a>, read directly <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.pa.gov/agencies/pgc/wildlife/discover-pa-wildlife/ruffed-grouse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pennsylvania Game Commission, Ruffed grouse</a>, read directly <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://idfg.idaho.gov/press/field-forest-grouse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Idaho Fish and Game</a>, read directly <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Audubon, dusky grouse; Colorado Parks and Wildlife; Birdweb sooty grouse; Herzog and Keppie 1980, read 2026-10-03 (65 to 70%).</li><li><a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/static/education/wns/grouse.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alaska Department of Fish and Game, grouse notebook</a>, search preview <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Hunt Map layers: app layer manifest, data dates in the map <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>.</li><li>All checked 2026-10-11 unless noted. Full log: <code>research/2026-10-11-masterclass-grouse.md</code>.</li></ul></details>"]